; G7 fixture — `printMulti`, the >=2-argument lowering of `print`.
;
; Why this file has to exist: `print(...)` lowers to `printMulti` only when it
; carries two or more arguments — one argument goes to `printCall` — and every
; other fixture in this directory prints a single value. Before this file the
; node had *no* corpus coverage at all: its only evidence was a synthetic
; single-node probe in `HIRExecutorTests.expressionGaps`, and grid G7 deleted
; that probe's entry when it implemented the node. Deleting without replacing
; would have traded a probe that misreports for a silence that reads like
; coverage.
;
; What is checked here: the lowering boundary (two arguments is exactly where
; `printCall` becomes `printMulti`), an arity past it, mixed scalar types, and
; the separator between operands — a space at the join is what the interpreter
; does (`outputSink(args.map { stringify($0) }.joined(separator: " "))`), and it
; is the one property of this node that a single-node probe could never check.
;
; **Scalar operands only, and that is a range statement rather than a
; shortcut.** An *aggregate* operand in a multi-argument print is a declared
; deferral on the LLVM arm: `emitPrintMulti` traps whenever an argument's type
; `hasNoScalarRendering`, emitting "printing an aggregate value is a later grid
; (value formatting)" and `unreachable`. That deferral is registered and closed
; as a **design boundary rather than a defect to route anywhere**, so leaving
; aggregates out of this file is a scope statement, not an omission. An operand
; like `[1, 2]` would abort `lli` and redden this harness for a reason owned
; elsewhere — the held-back `testDiffStructValue` shape, kept out for the same
; kind of reason. The scope of this file is the node, not the value formatter.
; (Measured, not assumed: the first draft printed `[1, 2]` alongside a string,
; and that is exactly how it failed.)
main|func() -> ():
    print("a", 1)
    print(true, "b", 2.5)
    print("tail")
    return
